    },
    {
      "id": "mizk9sd",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Archaeological Sources for Prehistoric Study"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "du6qjm5",
      "content": "Prehistory is reconstructed through material remains. Archaeologists rely on stratigraphy, typology of tools, carbon dating (where applicable), and comparative ethnography. - <strong>Stone tools </strong>indicate technological advancement. - <strong>Bones and fossils </strong>help reconstruct diet and physical features. - <strong>Cave paintings </strong>reflect cognitive and cultural development. - <strong>Habitational sites </strong>show settlement patterns."
    },
    {
      "id": "wlq13dc",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Environment and Climate in Prehistory"
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Feature",
        "Harappan Culture",
        "Chalcolithic Culture"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Nature of Settlement",
          "Urban, planned cities with grid patterns",
          "Rural settlements with mud and thatch houses"
        ],
        [
          "Metals Used",
          "Bronze and Copper predominantly",
          "Copper along with stone micro-blades"
        ],
        [
          "Pottery Style",
          "Red ware with black designs",
          "Painted Black-and-Red Ware (BRW)"
        ],
        [
          "Script & Literacy",
          "Pictographic script present",
          "Non-literate (No written script)"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "q55jepm"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "xk8kll4",
      "content": "Climatic changes played a major role in human evolution. - Paleolithic humans adapted to fluctuating climates. - Mesolithic phase coincides with post-Ice Age warming. - Stable climate in Neolithic period supported agriculture."
    },
